{"step_index":139,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T21:58:34Z","content":"A **\"Token (classic)\"** is GitHub's older, much simpler version of an access token (they start with `ghp_` instead of `github_pat_`). \n\nInstead of forcing you to individually select repositories and configure 20 different permission dropdowns (like the fine-grained token you just made), a classic token just has a simple checkbox.\n\nFor projects like this, it is much easier to just use a Classic token.\n\n**Here is exactly how to make one:**\n1. Go to **Settings** → **Developer Settings** on GitHub.\n2. On the left sidebar, click **Personal access tokens** → **Tokens (classic)**.\n3. Click **Generate new token** → **Generate new token (classic)**.\n4. Give it a name (e.g., \"Windows Local Repo\").\n5. Check the **`repo`** checkbox (this one checkbox gives it full access to push code to your repos).\n6. Scroll to the bottom and click **Generate token**.\n\nIt will give you a token starting with `ghp_...`. Just paste that new token here, and I will swap it out and push!","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Selection**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the importance of tool selection. My focus is sharpened; I must identify the most precise tool for each individual task before proceeding. I'm building out a list of related tools so that I can be very thoughtful about how to make tool calls. I need to get very particular about this.\n\n\n"}
